{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets every one of these acceptance lines:\n1. index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\n2. padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\n3. README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\n4. npm test passes\n\nCurrent state: index.js is an ES module (package.json has \"type\": \"module\") that exports capitalize, slugify and truncate. README.md has `## capitalize(s)` and `## truncate(s, n)` sections but NO section for slugify. Tests live in test/strutil.test.js and run with `npm test` (`node --test`).\n\nSteps:\n1. In index.js add and export `padLeft(s, width, fill = ' ')`, with a short doc comment like the other functions. It returns `s` with `fill` added on the left until the result is `width` characters long, e.g. padLeft('42', 5, '0') === '00042' and padLeft('7', 3) === '  7'. When s.length >= width, return s unchanged. Implementing it with `String.prototype.padStart(width, fill)` is fine.\n2. In README.md add a `## padLeft(s, width, fill)` section describing it (fill defaults to a space; s is returned unchanged when it is already width characters or longer).\n3. In README.md also add a `## slugify(s)` section (it is missing today): returns `s` lower-cased and trimmed, with runs of non-alphanumeric characters replaced by single dashes and no leading or trailing dash. Afterwards, check that every function exported from index.js has its own `## name(...)` section in README.md.\n4. In test/strutil.test.js import padLeft and add tests: padLeft('42', 5, '0') === '00042'; padLeft('7', 3) === '  7'; padLeft('12345', 5, '0') === '12345'; padLeft('123456', 5, '0') === '123456'. Keep the existing tests.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure it passes with no failures.\n6. Open the pull request, referencing https://github.com/o/strutil/issues/7.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run `npm install` if needed (there are no dependencies). Run one check per acceptance line, in this order.\n\nCheck 1 — \"index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\"\nRun from the repository root:\n`node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; const p=m.padLeft; console.log(JSON.stringify([typeof p, p('42',5,'0'), p('7',3), p('ab',4,'*')]))\"`\nPASS: it prints [\"function\",\"00042\",\"  7\",\"**ab\"], meaning padLeft is exported, pads on the left with the given fill to width characters, and uses a space when fill is not given.\nFAIL: the import errors, padLeft is not a function, or any value differs (wrong side, wrong length, or a non-space default fill).\n\nCheck 2 — \"padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\"\nRun:\n`node --input-type=module -e \"import {padLeft} from './index.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify([padLeft('12345',5,'0'), padLeft('123456',5,'0'), padLeft('abc',2), padLeft('abc',3)]))\"`\nPASS: it prints [\"12345\",\"123456\",\"abc\",\"abc\"].\nFAIL: any value differs from its input (padded, cut short or otherwise changed), or the command errors.\n\nCheck 3 — \"README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\"\nList the exported functions at runtime:\n`node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; console.log(Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function').join('\\n'))\"`\nThen read README.md and, for each name listed, look for a section (a markdown heading) for that function.\nPASS: every exported function name (expected to include at least capitalize, slugify, truncate and padLeft) has its own section in README.md.\nFAIL: any exported function has no section. Note: on the base branch slugify has no README section, so a PR that adds only a padLeft section fails this check.\n\nCheck 4 — \"npm test passes\"\nRun `npm test`.\nPASS: the test run completes with no failing tests.\nFAIL: any test fails, or the run errors.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS or FAIL):\n- Ideally `npm test` exits with status 0 and the test file includes padLeft tests covering both the padding case and the unchanged case.\n- Headings in the existing style (`## name(args)`) would be nice; any section heading naming the function is enough for check 3.\n- Behaviour with multi-character fill strings is not specified by the issue."}